## Comprehensive Summary
The sequestration of organic luminescent molecules (OLMs) within cage-based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as a dispersion platform has been developed to impede aggregation-caused quenching (ACQ). The homogenous encapsulation of distinct luminescent guests of different sizes and emissive behaviors in the cage structure of a MOF resulted in high fluorescent quantum yields of $44.8\%$ for DAPI@NKU-110 $(\mathsf{D A P l}\ =\ 4^{\prime},\mathsf{\Omega},$ 6-diamidino-2-phenylindole), $65.4\%$ for TPPA@NKU-110 (TPPA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=$ tris(4-(pyridin-4- yl)phenyl)amine), $31.3\%$ for $\mathbf{\mathsf{R6}G}@\mathbf{\mathsf{N}}\mathsf{K U}-\mathbf{\mathsf{110}}$ ( ${\mathsf{R}}6{\mathsf{G}}=$ Rhodamine 6G), and $58.3\%$ for $\mathsf{P Y@}$ NKU-110 ( $\mathsf{P}\mathsf{Y}=$ Pyronin Y), attributable to the confinement effect caused by the rigid cages of NKU-110. More significantly, a positive correlation of the encapsulated quantity of OLMs with their concentration in the in-situ solvothermal reaction was unveiled by spectral analysis and utilized to facilely fabricate a white-light-emitting crystal material TPPA+R6G $@$ NKU-110. This material features a large crystal size on the millimeter-scale, broadband white emission, ideal CIE coordinates (0.33, 0.34), and a high quantum yield $(49.1\%)$ when excited at $365~\mathsf{n m}$ . Moreover, such a strategy can be easily generalized to an abundance of other cage-based MOFs and a plentiful volume of OLMs for the future development of colorful, high performance luminescent materials.
Keywords
Metal-organic frameworks | Cage compounds | Fluorescence | Color tunability | In-situ encapsulation
## Background and Originality Content
Luminescent materials appear in daily life as displays, lighting, sensors, and imaging devices.[1] A model luminescent material should possess a wide emission spectrum, high quantum efficiency, and broad adjustability.[2] Traditional inorganic luminescent materials boast advantages such as large absorption coefficients, high quantum efficiencies, and high stability. However, their application is severely limited by single light style as well as poor adjustability and flexibility.[3] In contrast, organic luminescent materials (OLMs) have undergone rapid development attributable to their high color purity and ample tunability.[4] Unfortunately, the high emission intensities of most organic materials observed in dilute solutions, are apt to decline or even disappear in the solidstate due to the aggregation-caused quenching (ACQ) effect.[5] Though Tang’s group provides a way to design new solid organic luminescent materials based on the aggregation-induced emission (AIE) effect, a large number of materials continue to suffer from ACQ.[6] Organic molecules could exhibit highly useful luminescence properties if they could achieve a degree of molecular dispersion in the solid-state. It would be preferable to develop a general method to avoid the ACQ trap in the solid state for most reported organic luminescent molecules. One possible approach is to design a new solid dispersing medium for organic luminescent molecules, replacing liquid media in practical applications.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a promising class of crystalline inorganic-organic hybrid materials featuring uniform pores, high internal surface areas, and tailorable chemical structure and properties.[7-8] These advantages make MOFs unique hosts for adsorbing various functional guests, including metal ions,[9] organic molecules,[10] metal complexes,[11] polyoxometalate,[12] nanoparticles,[13] and proteins.[14] The resulting composite MOF materials offer opportunities for improved performances or emergent properties in comparison to the individual parent MOF and guest molecules separately.[15] Cage structures in crystalline MOFs evoke intensive interest because of their distinct cage structures with small window sizes and the potential to accommodate guest molecules.[16] With the uniform, separate, and tunable cavities in cage-based MOFs, rigid ‘cage effects’ may be exerted on incorporated OLMs.[17] Additionally, excellent luminescence properties of organic molecules in solid-state can be realized if they are dispersed at the molecular level. Well-ordered cages could shelter the encapsulated guests from interacting with each other, preventing organic molecules from losing energy through rotation.
At present, immersion methods are the prevalent means to include guests in MOFs.[18] However, results are restricted by many factors, such as the size and ionicity of guests and hosts.[19] If the pore size of a porous MOF is much large than the dimension of the guest molecule, it does little to restrict to the guest molecule, making it difficult to obtain a homogeneous, stable material. If the pore size of a porous MOF is smaller than or matched to the size of a guest molecule, the guest molecule will have difficulty in diffusing into the MOF. Therefore, the immersion method has huge limitations in encapsulating guest molecules. To overcome this problem, we have harnessed a promising in-situ self-assembly strategy, which not only has better tolerance to the size and charge of the guest molecules but also can make the guest molecules retain a stable, uniform dispersion. The most outstanding advantage of this method is that the concentration of the guest molecules in the in-situ reaction is in direct proportion to the concentration of the guest molecules that are immobilized into the MOF. Since the emission wavelength of the composite crystal relies on the number of guest molecules in the MOFs, it is easy for us to control the luminescent properties of the composite crystal by changing the concentration of the guest molecules. Although the in-situ encapsulation through one-pot reaction should be more effective, which requires much easy assembly of the cagebased MOFs even in the presence of diverse OLMs, it remains a great challenge to prepare excellent luminescent materials by in-situ encapsulation.
Motivated by the analyses mentioned above, we report the encapsulation of various OLMs in a cage-based MOF which acts as a solid solvent for preventing ${\mathsf{A C Q}},$ thereby creating single-crystalline materials with high fluorescence quantum yields (QY) and excellent luminescent properties. Herein, a cage-based MOF featuring appropriate confined spaces, $\mathsf{Z n}_{3}(\mathsf{P y C})(\mathsf{B T C})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T C})_{1/2}(\mathsf{O}^{2-})_{1/4}$ (denoted as NKU-110, $\mathsf{N K U}=$ Nankai University, $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}=1,2,3-$ benzenetricarboxylic acid, ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}\forall{\mathsf{C}}=4$ -pyrazolecarboxylic acid), was synthesized and served as an excellent host for encapsulating distinct guest molecules ranging in size and emission wavelength, DAPI (4',6-diamidino-2-phenylindole), TPPA (tris(4-(pyridin-4-yl)- phenyl)amine), R6G (Rhodamine 6G), and PY (Pyronin Y). The OLMs can be homogenously dispersed into the NKU-110 framework, which promises distinct emission from the blue to red regions and high QY due to the confinement effect derived from the internal cages. Furthermore, a good linear relation between the OLMs concentration used in the solvothermal reaction and the encapsulation quantity unveiled by spectral analysis was utilized to fabricate a white-light emitting hybrid material $(\mathsf{T P P A}+\mathsf{R6}\mathsf{G}@$ NKU-110). The resultant material exhibited broadband white light emission, ideal CIE coordinates (0.33, 0.34), and high QY $(49.11\%)$ excited at $365~\mathsf{n m}$ . This cage-based MOF provides a versatile platform for further functionalization and tunability.
## Results and Discussion
## Crystal structures
Single crystal X-ray structure analysis reveals that NKU-110 crystallizes in the $I\overline{{4}}_{3}d$ space group within the cubic crystal system with a formula of $\bar{Z^{\mathsf{n}}}_{3}(\bar{\mathsf{P}}\mathsf{\Psi}\mathsf{C})(\bar{\mathsf{B}}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{C}-\bar{\mathsf{1}})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{C}-2)_{1/2}(\mathsf{O}^{2-})_{1/4}$ (Figure S1). There are three crystallographically independent $Z n^{2+}$ ions in the asymmetric unit, all exhibiting tetrahedral coordination geometries. Each ${2}\mathsf{n}1^{2+}$ ion is coordinated to four ${\mathsf{B T C}}^{3-}$ ligands by four carboxylate oxygen atoms. Four ${2}\mathsf{n}1^{2+}$ ions are then bridged through these four carboxylate groups and a central $\mu_{4}–0$ atom to afford a tetranuclear tetrahedron molecular building block (MBB) (Figures 1a1 and S2a). The other two $Z n^{2+}$ ions, $Z n2^{2+}$ and $Z n3^{2+}$ , are connected through one N atom from $\mathsf{P y C}^{2-}$ and three carboxylate group oxygens. Four binuclear $Z n2^{2+}$ and four binuclear $Z n3^{2+}$ atoms are bound by eight carboxyl groups from four ${\mathsf{B T C}}^{3-}$ ligands, giving rise to a large ring-shaped structure (Figures 1a2 and S2b). Six ${\mathsf{B T C}}^{3-}$ ligands and one $\mu_{4}–0$ anion assemble with twelve zinc ions to generate an unprecedented dodecanuclear $Z n_{12}\mathrm{-}S B U$ , in which a classical $Z n_{4}0$ tetrahedral center is located in the center of the larger tetrahedron $Z n_{12}-S B U$ (Figures 1a3 and S2c). The six $Z n_{12}.5mathsf{B U s}$ are interconnected by nine $\mathsf{P y C}^{2-}$ ligands to generate an anti-trigonal mesoporous cage $Z\mathsf{n}_{72}(\mathsf{B T C})_{36}(\mathsf{P y C})_{9},$ featuring large free voids with a pore diameter of $27\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ surrounded by a large number of benzene rings (Figures 1b and S3). These anti-trigonal cages share surfaces or two edges between neighboring cages to afford an open 3D framework with pentagonal channels in four directions (Figures 1c and 1d). The entire 3D framework of NKU-110 can be viewed as a thp network when cages are simplified as 8-connected nodes (Figure S4). The effective free volume of NKU-110 is estimated to be $71.9\%$ using PLATON software $\boldsymbol{\lvert{49127.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}}}$ of the $68307.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}$ unit cell volume), upon removal of internal guest molecules. The phase purity of as-synthesized NKU-110 was confirmed by observation of a consistent match between experimental and simulated powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure S5). Thermogravimetric (TG) analyses showed that NKU-110 can retain its original structure up to $300^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Figure S6).
## Advantages of NKU-110 as a dispersion platform
Cage-based MOFs are considered excellent platforms for encapsulating OLMs to prevent ${\mathsf{A C Q}},$ owing to their large cavities and small window sizes that allow for the accommodation of varyingly sized guests while minimizing leaching. Based on the above structural analysis, the potential advantages of NKU-110 as a dispersion platform to incorporate OLMs and eliminate ACQ are summarized: 1) Mesoporous cages are formed through the connection of nanosized dodecanuclear clusters and linear linkers, providing the opportunity for guest molecule encapsulation. 2) The space surrounding the inner cavities is confined by multiple aromatic rings, forming a mesoporous cage-based framework with restrictive, small apertures. Due to this specific structure, OLMs can enter the MOFs by the means of an in-situ reaction and once they are restrained within the cavity, they have a decreased likelihood to escape. Moreover, aromatic rings enveloping the cavity can easily stabilize these OLMs through π-π interactions. 3) The high thermodynamic stability of NKU-110 suggests that a diverse array of OLMs can be incorporated without hindering the MOF’s formation. 4) Lastly, NKU-110 can be prepared in mild reaction conditions with high yields, an essential condition for practical application.
Most importantly, shape and size play a crucial role in the fluorescence capacity of crystalline fluorescent materials. Because of anisotropic differences in mixed crystalline materials, some single crystals have difficulty in expressing their intrinsic fluorescent capacities. Thus, it is significant to obtain single-crystal materials in the study of crystalline fluorescent materials. Additionally, related crystalline materials reported in the literature are often too small to meet the testing requirements, limiting their applicability. Compared to these tiny single-crystal materials, the herein obtained fluorescent materials are all single-crystal materials with sizes on the millimeter scale, meeting the prerequisites for the device and application of fluorescent materials.
## Preparation of OLM@NKU-110
In this work, we utilize NKU-110 to incorporate OLMs through an in-situ self-assembly strategy, generating high performance solid-state luminescent materials by dispersing the OLMs and hence impeding ACQ. Four OLMs featuring different sizes and emission wavelengths, DAPI, TPPA, R6G, and PY, were selected to be encapsulated during solvothermal synthesis of NKU-110. The colors of the resulting samples support the notion that a diverse array of guest molecules may be successfully incorporated into the framework by this simple method (Figure 2). To confirm the consistency of these resulting structures with crystalline samples of NKU-110, PXRD was performed. As evidenced by the PXRD data, the crystallinity and framework structure of the OLMs-incorporated crystals were maintained (Figures S7—S11). These crystals were named DAPI@NKU-110, TPPA@NKU-110, R6G@ NKU-110, and PY@NKU-110, respectively.
## Photoluminescent properties and tuning of emission colors
Photoluminescence spectra of NKU-110, DAPI, TPPA, R6G, PY,
DAPI@NKU-110, TPPA@NKU-110, R6G@NKU-110, and PY@NKU110 were studied at room temperature. NKU-110 shows a comparatively narrow purple/blue emission band with an intense fluorescent emission maximum at $385~\mathsf{n m}$ when excited at 340 nm (Figures S12 and S13). The solutions of DAPI, TPPA, R6G, and PY in $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, $10^{-5}~\mathsf{m o l/L})$ exhibit strong blue, cyan, orange, and red emission spectra around $440\mathsf{n m}(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=$ $350~\mathsf{n m}$ ), $455\ \mathsf{n m}$ $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=355~\mathsf{n m})$ , $565~\mathsf{n m}$ $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=510\ \mathsf{n m})$ and 576 nm $(\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=520\ \mathrm{nm})$ , respectively (Figures S14—S17). For $\mathsf{O L M@}$ NKU-110 samples, the emission spectra are not only guest-dependent but also concentration-dependent. The concentration of the guest molecules in the in-situ reaction is directly proportional to the concentration of the guest molecules immobilized into the MOFs (Figures S18—S25). The maximum emission wavelength of fluorescence towards NKU-110 also positively correlates with the guest concentration. As shown in Figure 3a, the emission peaks of the DAPI $@$ NKU-110 composite gradually undergo red-shift from
$430\mathsf{n m}$ to $465\mathsf{n m}$ upon increasing the concentration of DAPI. The fluorescence of TPPA $@$ NKU-110 was also easily observed to redshift from $470\ \mathsf{n m}$ to $540~\mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 3b). Similarly, red-shifts from $560~\mathsf{n m}$ to $590~\mathsf{n m}$ and from $585~\mathsf{n m}$ to $620~\mathsf{n m}$ for ${\mathsf{R6}}{\mathsf{G}}@{\mathsf{N}}{\mathsf{K U-}}$ 110 (Figure 3c) and PY@NKU-110 (Figure 3d) were observed, respectively. We found that the concentration of the guest molecules in the in-situ reaction is in direct proportion to the concentration of the MOF-immobilized guest molecules. This change of luminescence spectra also indicates that the OLMs have been encapsulated into the cages successfully. The emission bands of OLMs gradually red-shift with increasing OLM content, which may be attributed to reabsorption effects and/or dye aggregation at higher concentrations.[20]
## Interactive forces between OLMs and NKU-110 in OLM@ NKU-110
To investigate the emissive behaviors of OLM@NKU-110 and the interaction between the encapsulated OLM molecules and NKU-110, confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM), solid-state fluorescence spectroscopy, and UV-Vis absorption spectroscopy were carried out. The CLSM images of DAPI@NKU-110, TPPA@ NKU-110, R6G@NKU-110, and PY@NKU-110 show that the four organic molecules are homogenously dispersed into the NKU-110 framework (Figure 4), further suggesting the advantages of NKU110 as a host to encapsulate dyes in the form of in-situ assembly. To characterize the emissive behaviors of OLM@NKU-110, the photo-luminescence mechanism was investigated based on fluorescence spectroscopy and UV-Vis absorption spectroscopy. The spectral overlap between the absorption of DAPI, TPPA, R6G, and PY and the emission of NKU-110 facilitates efficient energy transfer from NKU-110 to the OLMs (Figures S26—S29).[21] The energy transfer was also confirmed by steady state emissions of the OLM@NKU-110. Upon excitation of NKU-110 at $340\ \mathsf{n m}$ , $\mathsf{O L M@}$ NKU-110 shows a comparatively wider band fluorescence emission spectrum with two emission peaks corresponding to the emission of NKU-110 and the emission of OLMs resulting from energy transfer (Figures S30—S32).
The space confinement of cage structure within NKU-111 not only prevents the aggregation of organic molecules, but also reduces the energy loss derived from rotations or vibrations. In the crystalline state, organic molecules are in a state that is similar to diffusing in the solution, which maintains the high luminescence efficiency in the solid perfectly. The QYs of OLM $@$ NKU-110 with different OLM content are shown in Table 1. OLM $@$ NKU-110 exhibits different QYs according to the amount of OLMs employed. The QYs of DAPI@NKU-110, TPPA@NKU-110, and R6G@NKU-110 first increase and then decrease with an increasing amount of OLMs incorporated in NKU-110, while PY@NKU-110 has the highest QY when the lowest amount of PY is placed in the framework. The DAPI $@110$ , TPPA $@$ NKU-110, R6G $@$ NKU-110, and PY@NKU-110 display their highest QY of $44.8\%$ , $65.4\%$ , $31.3\%$ , and $58.9\%$ when $100~\upmu\upiota$ , $50~\upmu\up L$ , $2500~\upmu\mathrm{L},$ and $100~\upmu\upiota$ guest molecules were added to the reaction system, respectively. The highest quantum efficiency of DAP $@$ NKU-110, TPPA@NKU-110, ${\mathsf{R}}6{\mathsf{G}}({\varpi}$ NKU-110, and PY@NKU-110 correspond to blue, yellow, orange, and red-light emissions, respectively, which are outstanding in solid luminescent materials. The trend of the QY presented in these solid-state solvents is consistent with the trend of changes in the QY of OLMs in different solutions, demonstrating that cage-based MOFs work well as solid solvents. In addition, we compared the solid-state QY of OLMs and in-situ packaged $\mathsf{O L M@}$ NKU-110. The in-situ packed OLM $@$ NKU-110 has significantly improved solid-state QY (Figure S33). Moreover, the more significant the ACQ effect of OLMs, the greater the QY of OLM@NKU110 compared to OLMs. We also prepared a series of $\mathsf{o L M}\in$ NKU-110 materials with different amounts of OLMs by mechanical mixing. The QY of the ${\mathsf{O L M}}\in{\mathsf{N K U}}{\cdot}110$ material prepared by grinding is improved when compared to OLMs, but the effect is not substantial (Table S3). Therefore, it was determined that the in-situ self-assembly strategy is a good way to prepare high QY solid-state fluorescent materials.
## Fabrication of a multicolored LED
Solid-state white-light-emitting diodes (WLEDs) have aroused great interest in the subjects of illumination, displays, and indicators due to their energy conservation, high efficiency, and long lifetime.[22] Presently, the regulation of emission color can be a sophisticated and complicated pursuit and it remains a great challenge to rationally fabricate white-light-emitting materials.[23] In the in-situ self-assembly strategy, we found that the concentration of the OLMs in the in-situ reaction is in direct proportion to the concentration of the MOF-immobilized OLMs. Since the emission wavelength of the composite crystal relies on the number of OLMs within the MOF, modifications to the OLM concentration should control the emission color of these crystals. Considering that TPPA@NKU-110 and R6G@NKU-110 are excellent cyan and orange emitters, respectively, we attempted to encapsulate TPPA and R6G into NKU-110 simultaneously to fabricate a white-lightemitting material. TPPA and R6G were simultaneously introduced into the solvothermal synthesis of NKU-110. As a result, TPPA and R6G were successfully incorporated into the framework together. In a more encouraging sign, the concentration of the OLMs in the in-situ reaction is in direct proportion to the concentration of the immobilized OLMs even when two kinds of OLMs are present. Through this, we can rationally fabricate white-light-emitting materials by simply adjusting the amount of OLMs in the reaction system (Figure S34). The targeted white-light emission was obtained when $200~\upmu\upiota$ and $170\upmu\upiota$ of TPPA and R6G $(3\ m\ m\/\ell)$ were inserted into the reaction system, respectively. Under these conditions, $0.026\mathrm{\wt\%}$ of TPPA and 0.011 wt% of R6G were encapsulated in NKU-110. Upon excitation at $365~\mathsf{n m}$ , a broadband emission with two emission peaks corresponding to the emission of TPPA and the emission of R6G can be achieved (Figure 5). The corresponding CIE chromaticity coordinates were calculated to be (0.33, 0.34), which are very close to those of ideal white light (0.33, 0.33). The absolute QY was measured as $49.11\%$ by exciting the samples with diffuse light within an integrating sphere. These values are far better than the reported white-light-emitting MOFs. It is worth remembering that the QY was measured at ambient temperature and atmospheric pressure, which is likely closer to practical working application conditions. To the best of our knowledge, the QY of TPPA+R6G@NKU-110 is the highest among all of white-light-emitting dye@MOFs reported to date excited at 365 nm.[11a,17a,19,21b,24] Meanwhile, multi-colored LEDs showing white, cyan, and yellow were fabricated by coating PPA+R6G@NKU-110, TPPA $@$ NKU-110, and R6G@NKU-110 on the 365 nm UV-LED chip, respectively (Figure S35). Furthermore, the logo of Nankai University composed of TPPA+R6G@NKU-110, TPPA@NKU-110, and R6G@NKU-110 emits white, cyan, and yellow light under 365 nm UV light source. More importantly, the TPPA+R6G@NKU-110 composite exhibits good thermostability and photostability (Figures S36 and S37).
## Conclusions
## Experimental
Materials and methods. All chemicals were purchased and used without further purification. Measurements of powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data were carried out on a Rigaku D/Max-2500 diffractometer. Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) were carried out on a Rigaku standard TGDTA analyzer under ${\sf N}_{2}$ with a heating rate of $10\mathrm{^{~\circ}C/m i n}$ . The UV−vis spectra were recorded by a SHIMADZU UV-3600 spectrophotometer at room temperature (RT). Photoluminescence (PL) measurements were performed on
Hitachi F4500 fluorescence spectrophotometers. The emission quantum yields of samples were measured with an Edinburgh FLS980 fluorescence spectrophotometer under air at room temperature. Fluorescence images of the crystals were taken on an automated Leica DM5000B microscope equipped with different filter cubes.
Single crystal X-ray crystallography. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction data for NKU-110 was collected on a Rigaku SuperNova CCD diffractometer at $160~\mathsf{K}$ with $c_{u-K\alpha}$ radiation $(\lambda=1.54184\mathrm{~\AA})$ in multi-scan mode. The structure of NKU-110 was solved by direct methods and the full-matrix least-square method was used to refine the crystal structure. All non-hydrogen atoms were refined through anisotropic displacement parameters, and all hydrogen atoms were generated theoretically based on the calculated positions. Detailed crystallographic data and structure refinement details for NKU-110 are listed in Table S1, and selected bond lengths and angles are given in Table S2. The CCDC number for NKU-110 is 2110243.
Synthesis of NKU-110. $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}G H_{2}O$ $\left(0.5\mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ , ${\sf H}_{2}\sf P\sf v{\sf c}$ (0.2 mmol), $H_{3}B T C$ $(0.25~\mathsf{m m o l})$ , DMF $(5~\mathsf{m L})$ , and $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylacetamide (DMA, $5m L$ ) were mixed and ultrasonically treated until completely dissolved. The mixture was transferred and sealed into a $20~\mathsf{m L}$ vial and heated at $95^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $72\ h$ . After natural cooling to ambient temperature, colorless polyhedral crystals were obtained. The resulting products were rinsed with fresh DMF and ethanol several times and then dried in air (Yield: $90\%$ , based on ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf B T C})$ .
Synthesis of DAPI@NKU-110. $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O$ $(0.5~\mathrm{\mmol})$ , ${\sf H}_{2}\sf P\forall C$ (0.2 mmol), $H_{3}B\mathsf{T C}$ $\left(0.25\mathrm{\mmol}\right)_{i}$ , DMF $(5~\mathsf{m L})$ , and DMA (5 mL) were mixed and ultrasonically treated until completely dissolved, then a series of different volumes of DAPI solution (3 mmol/L) were added to it. The mixtures were transferred and sealed into a $20~\mathsf{m L}$ vial and heated at $95^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $72\textmu$ . After natural cooling to ambient temperature, colorless polyhedral crystals were obtained. The resulting products were rinsed with fresh DMF and ethanol several times and then dried in air (Yield: $92\%$ , based on ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf B T C})$ .
Synthesis of TPPA@NKU-110, R6G@NKU-110, and PY@NKU110: The synthesis processes for TPPA@NKU-110, R6G@NKU-110, and PY@NKU-110 are similar to those for DAPI@NKU-110, except that the DAPI solution was replaced by TPPA, R6G, and PY.
